1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,222
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:04,222 --> 00:00:08,444
Principles, Big Idea 3, part one. We'll
learn the building blocks of every

3
00:00:08,444 --> 00:00:13,008
program, written in AP exam pseudocode.
After every lesson, we'll work through a

4
00:00:13,008 --> 00:00:17,515
full example step by step, and we'll
finish by putting the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,688 --> 00:00:24,064
Big Idea 3 is the biggest part of the AP
exam: algorithms and programming.

6
00:00:24,514 --> 00:00:25,965
And it starts with variables.

7
00:00:27,115 --> 00:00:32,749
A variable is a named place that holds a
value. On the AP exam, assignment uses a

8
00:00:32,749 --> 00:00:33,515
left arrow.

9
00:00:33,965 --> 00:00:37,698
a, arrow, five, stores five in a. You read
it as a gets five.

10
00:00:38,848 --> 00:00:44,178
A variable holds one value at a time.
Assigning a new value replaces the old

11
00:00:44,178 --> 00:00:44,459
one.

12
00:00:44,909 --> 00:00:48,493
a gets five. Then a gets eight. Now a is
eight, and five is gone.

13
00:00:49,643 --> 00:00:52,480
The right side is evaluated first, then
stored.

14
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:57,797
score gets score plus ten takes the
current score, adds ten, and stores the

15
00:00:57,797 --> 00:00:59,159
result back in score.

16
00:01:00,309 --> 00:01:05,900
Watch out for this classic exam question.
a gets one, b gets two, a gets b, b gets

17
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,264
a. What are a and b?

18
00:01:07,714 --> 00:01:12,933
a becomes two. Then b gets a, which is now
two. Both are two. To swap, you need a

19
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:14,157
temporary variable.

20
00:01:15,307 --> 00:01:18,421
Choose meaningful names, like highScore
instead of x.

21
00:01:18,871 --> 00:01:23,181
Good names make code easier to read, for
you and for anyone you work with.

22
00:01:24,331 --> 00:01:30,432
Your turn. x gets three. y gets x plus
two. x gets y times two. What are x and y?

23
00:01:30,882 --> 00:01:32,077
Pause the video now.

24
00:01:32,527 --> 00:01:38,244
y is five. Then x is five times two, which
is ten. So x is ten and y is five.

25
00:01:38,694 --> 00:01:41,681
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

26
00:01:42,831 --> 00:01:47,524
Let's trace assignments in AP pseudocode,
and catch a trap the exam loves.

27
00:01:48,674 --> 00:01:53,309
The problem: what does this code display?
Watch what happens to total when price

28
00:01:53,309 --> 00:01:53,773
changes.

29
00:01:54,923 --> 00:01:59,296
The arrow means gets. The right side is
worked out once, right then, and stored.

30
00:01:59,746 --> 00:02:03,138
Changing price later does not go back and
change total.

31
00:02:04,288 --> 00:02:06,848
price gets twenty, qty gets three.

32
00:02:07,298 --> 00:02:09,645
total gets twenty times three: sixty.

33
00:02:10,095 --> 00:02:15,275
price gets twenty five. total still holds
sixty. It doesn't recalculate. Display

34
00:02:15,275 --> 00:02:15,663
sixty.

35
00:02:16,813 --> 00:02:19,586
The program displays sixty, not seventy
five.

36
00:02:20,036 --> 00:02:25,956
A variable holds a value, not a formula.
To update total, you'd need to run total

37
00:02:25,956 --> 00:02:27,930
gets price times qty again.

38
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:32,024
Your turn. Trace these four lines. What is
displayed?

39
00:02:32,474 --> 00:02:33,668
Pause the video now.

40
00:02:34,118 --> 00:02:36,721
Three. b got a copy of three before a
changed.

41
00:02:37,171 --> 00:02:38,046
Next, lists.

42
00:02:39,195 --> 00:02:43,662
What if a program needs a hundred test
scores? A hundred variables would be a

43
00:02:43,662 --> 00:02:43,952
mess.

44
00:02:44,402 --> 00:02:45,576
A list solves that.

45
00:02:46,726 --> 00:02:51,495
A list is an ordered collection of
elements. On the AP exam it's written with

46
00:02:51,495 --> 00:02:52,486
square brackets.

47
00:02:52,936 --> 00:02:55,453
scores gets eighty five, ninety, seventy
two.

48
00:02:56,603 --> 00:03:01,531
Here's a big difference from Python. AP
lists start at index one, not zero.

49
00:03:01,981 --> 00:03:06,738
scores, bracket one, is eighty five.
scores, bracket three, is seventy two.

50
00:03:07,888 --> 00:03:12,240
Using an index outside the list, like
scores bracket four, is an error.

51
00:03:12,690 --> 00:03:16,993
LENGTH of scores gives the number of
elements, so the last index is always

52
00:03:16,993 --> 00:03:17,981
LENGTH of scores.

53
00:03:19,131 --> 00:03:23,642
Lists are a kind of data abstraction. You
give one name to a whole collection, and

54
00:03:23,642 --> 00:03:25,787
you don't need to know how it's stored.

55
00:03:26,237 --> 00:03:29,458
That makes programs simpler to write, and
easier to change.

56
00:03:30,608 --> 00:03:35,542
Strings and lists are both ordered. Each
element of a list can even be a string,

57
00:03:35,542 --> 00:03:36,838
like a list of names.

58
00:03:37,988 --> 00:03:43,300
Your turn. nums gets ten, twenty, thirty,
forty. What is nums bracket two, and what

59
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:44,452
is LENGTH of nums?

60
00:03:44,902 --> 00:03:46,096
Pause the video now.

61
00:03:46,546 --> 00:03:51,261
nums bracket two is twenty, because
indexes start at one. LENGTH is four.

62
00:03:51,711 --> 00:03:54,698
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

63
00:03:55,848 --> 00:04:01,437
Let's trace a list through every AP list
procedure, remembering that AP lists start

64
00:04:01,437 --> 00:04:02,312
at index one.

65
00:04:03,462 --> 00:04:08,736
The problem: scores starts as eighty five,
ninety, seventy two, sixty four. Show the

66
00:04:08,736 --> 00:04:10,054
list after each line.

67
00:04:11,204 --> 00:04:16,404
Index one is the first element. INSERT
puts a value at an index and shifts the

68
00:04:16,404 --> 00:04:20,271
rest right. REMOVE takes one out and
shifts the rest left.

69
00:04:21,421 --> 00:04:25,687
scores at two is the second element,
ninety. It becomes ninety five.

70
00:04:26,137 --> 00:04:30,729
APPEND adds eighty eight at the end.
INSERT puts seventy at index one, so

71
00:04:30,729 --> 00:04:32,239
everything shifts right.

72
00:04:32,689 --> 00:04:37,097
REMOVE at three takes out the third
element, ninety five. Five elements

73
00:04:37,097 --> 00:04:37,531
remain.

74
00:04:38,681 --> 00:04:43,716
The list ends as seventy, eighty five,
seventy two, sixty four, eighty eight, and

75
00:04:43,716 --> 00:04:45,145
the display shows five.

76
00:04:45,595 --> 00:04:50,737
Using index zero, or an index bigger than
the length, is an error on the AP exam.

77
00:04:51,887 --> 00:04:55,236
Your turn. After all that, what is scores
at LENGTH of scores?

78
00:04:55,686 --> 00:04:56,881
Pause the video now.

79
00:04:57,331 --> 00:04:58,803
Eighty eight, the last element.

80
00:04:59,253 --> 00:05:01,343
Next, math expressions and MOD.

81
00:05:02,493 --> 00:05:08,967
Programs calculate constantly. The AP exam
uses the familiar operators, plus one that

82
00:05:08,967 --> 00:05:10,642
surprises people: MOD.

83
00:05:11,792 --> 00:05:17,771
Plus, minus, times and divide work as
usual. In AP pseudocode, divide gives the

84
00:05:17,771 --> 00:05:22,160
exact answer, so seven divided by two is
three point five.

85
00:05:22,610 --> 00:05:26,691
Order of operations applies:
multiplication and division before

86
00:05:26,691 --> 00:05:29,800
addition and subtraction, and parentheses
first.

87
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:34,747
a MOD b gives the remainder when a is
divided by b.

88
00:05:35,197 --> 00:05:40,913
Seventeen MOD five is two, because five
goes into seventeen three times, with two

89
00:05:40,913 --> 00:05:41,618
left over.

90
00:05:42,768 --> 00:05:46,800
MOD has the same priority as
multiplication and division.

91
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:51,325
So three plus ten MOD four is three plus
two, which is five.

92
00:05:52,475 --> 00:05:58,854
MOD is very useful. n MOD two is zero for
even numbers and one for odd numbers.

93
00:05:59,304 --> 00:06:02,098
And n MOD ten gives the last digit of n.

94
00:06:03,248 --> 00:06:08,874
MOD also wraps around. To go around a
clock, hour gets hour plus one, MOD

95
00:06:08,874 --> 00:06:09,414
twelve.

96
00:06:10,564 --> 00:06:16,190
Your turn. What is twenty three MOD four,
and what is four plus six times two MOD

97
00:06:16,190 --> 00:06:16,537
five?

98
00:06:16,987 --> 00:06:18,182
Pause the video now.

99
00:06:18,632 --> 00:06:24,527
Twenty three MOD four is three. Then six
times two is twelve, twelve MOD five is

100
00:06:24,527 --> 00:06:26,738
two, and four plus two is six.

101
00:06:27,188 --> 00:06:30,175
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

102
00:06:31,325 --> 00:06:36,859
Let's use MOD to solve three real
wrap-around problems: a clock, a calendar,

103
00:06:36,859 --> 00:06:38,024
and odd or even.

104
00:06:39,174 --> 00:06:44,036
The problem: it's ten o'clock. What time
is it five hours later on a twelve hour

105
00:06:44,036 --> 00:06:44,400
clock?

106
00:06:44,850 --> 00:06:50,617
Today is Saturday, day six, with Sunday as
day zero. What day is it in ten days? And

107
00:06:50,617 --> 00:06:51,784
is seventeen odd?

108
00:06:52,934 --> 00:06:56,518
a MOD b is the remainder when a is divided
by b.

109
00:06:56,968 --> 00:07:03,185
MOD is perfect for anything that wraps
around, like hours and days. MOD two tells

110
00:07:03,185 --> 00:07:04,413
you odd or even.

111
00:07:05,563 --> 00:07:11,180
Ten plus five is fifteen. Fifteen divided
by twelve is one, remainder three. Three

112
00:07:11,180 --> 00:07:11,728
o'clock.

113
00:07:12,178 --> 00:07:18,191
Six plus ten is sixteen. Sixteen divided
by seven is two, remainder two. Day two is

114
00:07:18,191 --> 00:07:18,770
Tuesday.

115
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:23,722
Seventeen divided by two leaves one. So
seventeen is odd: true.

116
00:07:24,872 --> 00:07:26,536
Three o'clock, Tuesday, and odd.

117
00:07:26,986 --> 00:07:32,654
Common mistake: thinking MOD gives the
quotient. Fifteen MOD twelve is three, the

118
00:07:32,654 --> 00:07:33,983
remainder, not one.

119
00:07:35,133 --> 00:07:39,762
Your turn. What is twenty three MOD five,
and five MOD twenty three?

120
00:07:40,212 --> 00:07:41,407
Pause the video now.

121
00:07:41,857 --> 00:07:46,242
Three, and five. When the first number is
smaller, the remainder is the number

122
00:07:46,242 --> 00:07:46,636
itself.

123
00:07:47,086 --> 00:07:48,110
Next, strings.

124
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:53,313
Names, messages, passwords. Programs work
with text all the time.

125
00:07:53,763 --> 00:07:55,235
Text is stored as strings.

126
00:07:56,385 --> 00:07:59,863
A string is an ordered sequence of
characters, written in quotes.

127
00:08:00,313 --> 00:08:02,297
The string hello has five characters.

128
00:08:03,447 --> 00:08:05,879
Concatenation joins strings end to end.

129
00:08:06,329 --> 00:08:11,377
Concatenating sun and flower gives
sunflower. No space is added unless you

130
00:08:11,377 --> 00:08:12,195
include one.

131
00:08:13,345 --> 00:08:15,095
A substring is part of a string.

132
00:08:15,545 --> 00:08:20,533
On the AP exam, a substring is often
described by a starting position and a

133
00:08:20,533 --> 00:08:25,788
length. Starting at position one, with
length three, the substring of sunflower

134
00:08:25,788 --> 00:08:26,254
is sun.

135
00:08:27,404 --> 00:08:31,372
Like lists, strings in AP pseudocode count
positions from one.

136
00:08:31,822 --> 00:08:35,534
The number of characters is the length.
sunflower has length nine.

137
00:08:36,684 --> 00:08:41,198
Strings and numbers are different types.
The string five and the number five are

138
00:08:41,198 --> 00:08:41,932
not the same.

139
00:08:42,382 --> 00:08:46,179
Joining the strings one and two gives the
string one two, not three.

140
00:08:47,329 --> 00:08:52,185
Your turn. Concatenate code and run. Then
take the substring starting at position

141
00:08:52,185 --> 00:08:54,583
three with length four. What do you get?

142
00:08:55,033 --> 00:08:56,227
Pause the video now.

143
00:08:56,677 --> 00:09:02,227
coderun. Starting at position three, four
characters: d, e, r, u. So the answer is

144
00:09:02,227 --> 00:09:02,565
deru.

145
00:09:03,015 --> 00:09:06,002
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

146
00:09:07,152 --> 00:09:12,037
Let's build a username with concatenation
and substrings, AP exam style.

147
00:09:13,187 --> 00:09:17,438
The problem: make a username from the
first three letters of the first name,

148
00:09:17,438 --> 00:09:21,913
plus the first three letters of the last
name, plus the length of the full name.

149
00:09:22,363 --> 00:09:26,992
The exam defines substring of str, start,
length, counting from one.

150
00:09:28,142 --> 00:09:32,707
substring of first, one, three, takes
three characters starting at position one.

151
00:09:33,157 --> 00:09:36,805
concat joins strings end to end. No spaces
are added.

152
00:09:37,955 --> 00:09:40,259
substring of Maya, one, three: M a y.

153
00:09:40,709 --> 00:09:43,419
substring of Rodriguez, one, three: R o d.

154
00:09:43,869 --> 00:09:48,647
Four letters plus nine letters is
thirteen. Join them all: May Rod thirteen.

155
00:09:49,797 --> 00:09:51,845
The username is May Rod thirteen.

156
00:09:52,295 --> 00:09:57,730
On the exam, always read how the question
defines its string procedures. Positions

157
00:09:57,730 --> 00:09:59,122
usually start at one.

158
00:10:00,272 --> 00:10:03,216
Your turn. What is substring of sunflower,
four, six?

159
00:10:03,666 --> 00:10:04,861
Pause the video now.

160
00:10:05,311 --> 00:10:08,937
flower. Start at position four, the f, and
take six characters.

161
00:10:09,387 --> 00:10:10,923
Next, Boolean expressions.

162
00:10:12,073 --> 00:10:16,574
Every decision a program makes comes down
to one question: true or false?

163
00:10:17,024 --> 00:10:18,411
Those are Boolean values.

164
00:10:19,561 --> 00:10:21,844
A Boolean value is either true or false.

165
00:10:22,294 --> 00:10:27,723
Relational operators compare values and
give a Boolean: equals, not equal, greater

166
00:10:27,723 --> 00:10:31,232
than, less than, and greater or equal,
less or equal.

167
00:10:32,382 --> 00:10:38,057
On the AP exam, equals is a single equals
sign, because assignment uses the arrow.

168
00:10:38,507 --> 00:10:42,283
And not equal is written as an equals sign
with a slash through it.

169
00:10:43,433 --> 00:10:45,673
NOT flips a Boolean. NOT true is false.

170
00:10:46,123 --> 00:10:51,094
AND is true only if both sides are true.
OR is true if at least one side is true.

171
00:10:52,244 --> 00:10:57,735
To check if age is a teenager: age greater
than or equal to thirteen, AND age less

172
00:10:57,735 --> 00:10:59,476
than or equal to nineteen.

173
00:10:59,926 --> 00:11:01,355
Both parts must be true.

174
00:11:02,505 --> 00:11:07,900
NOT is applied before AND, and AND before
OR. When in doubt, use parentheses to make

175
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:08,478
it clear.

176
00:11:09,628 --> 00:11:15,740
Your turn. x is seven. Is this true or
false: x greater than five AND x MOD two

177
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:16,668
equals zero?

178
00:11:17,118 --> 00:11:18,313
Pause the video now.

179
00:11:18,763 --> 00:11:23,835
Seven is greater than five, true. But
seven MOD two is one, so that part is

180
00:11:23,835 --> 00:11:25,931
false. True AND false is false.

181
00:11:26,381 --> 00:11:29,368
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

182
00:11:30,518 --> 00:11:35,702
Let's evaluate a Boolean expression with
AND, OR and NOT for three real visitors.

183
00:11:36,852 --> 00:11:42,332
The problem: a science museum's late night
event is open to visitors aged sixteen or

184
00:11:42,332 --> 00:11:45,855
older who have a ticket, or to anyone who
is a member.

185
00:11:46,305 --> 00:11:47,201
Who gets in?

186
00:11:48,351 --> 00:11:53,151
AND is true only when both sides are true.
OR is true when at least one side is.

187
00:11:53,601 --> 00:11:55,265
Work out the parentheses first.

188
00:11:56,415 --> 00:12:01,631
Visitor one: seventeen, with a ticket, not
a member. Both parts of the AND are true,

189
00:12:01,631 --> 00:12:02,687
so true. Gets in.

190
00:12:03,137 --> 00:12:07,951
Visitor two: fourteen, with a ticket, not
a member. The AND is false. Not a member.

191
00:12:07,951 --> 00:12:08,299
False.

192
00:12:08,749 --> 00:12:13,870
Visitor three: twelve, no ticket, but a
member. The AND is false, but OR member is

193
00:12:13,870 --> 00:12:14,744
true. Gets in.

194
00:12:15,894 --> 00:12:17,515
Visitors one and three get in.

195
00:12:17,965 --> 00:12:22,104
NOT flips a value. NOT canEnter would be
true only for visitor two.

196
00:12:23,254 --> 00:12:28,235
Your turn. What is NOT, open parenthesis,
five greater than three, AND two equals

197
00:12:28,235 --> 00:12:28,481
two?

198
00:12:28,931 --> 00:12:30,125
Pause the video now.

199
00:12:30,575 --> 00:12:34,714
False. Both comparisons are true, so the
AND is true, and NOT flips it.

200
00:12:35,164 --> 00:12:36,871
Next, let's put Part 1 together.

201
00:12:38,021 --> 00:12:42,736
You've learned variables, lists, math with
MOD, strings and Boolean expressions.

202
00:12:43,186 --> 00:12:48,722
Now let's write one complete program in AP
pseudocode that uses every one, and run

203
00:12:48,722 --> 00:12:48,924
it.

204
00:12:50,074 --> 00:12:52,122
Here's the plan: a savings tracker.

205
00:12:52,572 --> 00:12:57,465
It stores how many cents Maya saved each
day, totals them, shows the amount in

206
00:12:57,465 --> 00:12:59,911
dollars and cents, and checks the goal.

207
00:13:01,061 --> 00:13:05,690
Part one: a list of cents saved each day,
and a goal of nine hundred cents.

208
00:13:06,140 --> 00:13:11,079
FOR EACH adds every element to total, the
accumulator pattern. You'll study the loop

209
00:13:11,079 --> 00:13:13,372
in Part 2, but you can already read it.

210
00:13:14,522 --> 00:13:17,914
Part two: MOD one hundred gives the
leftover cents.

211
00:13:18,364 --> 00:13:22,930
Subtracting the cents first makes the
division exact, so dollars is a whole

212
00:13:22,930 --> 00:13:23,356
number.

213
00:13:24,506 --> 00:13:29,335
Part three: concat builds the message.
reached is a Boolean: the total meets the

214
00:13:29,335 --> 00:13:31,568
goal, and it took at most seven days.

215
00:13:32,018 --> 00:13:35,772
saved at four is the fourth day, because
AP lists start at one.

216
00:13:36,922 --> 00:13:41,514
Let's run it. The total is nine hundred
thirty cents: nine dollars and thirty

217
00:13:41,514 --> 00:13:41,872
cents.

218
00:13:42,322 --> 00:13:47,097
That's at least nine hundred, in five
days, so reached is true. And day four was

219
00:13:47,097 --> 00:13:49,127
the best day, three hundred cents.

220
00:13:50,277 --> 00:13:53,541
Your turn. If the goal were one thousand,
what would reached be?

221
00:13:53,991 --> 00:13:55,186
Pause the video now.

222
00:13:55,636 --> 00:13:58,580
False. Nine hundred thirty is less than
one thousand.

223
00:13:59,030 --> 00:14:03,915
That's Part 1. Next, Part 2: conditionals,
iteration, and list algorithms.

224
00:14:05,065 --> 00:14:09,494
If this helped, subscribe to School STEM,
Explained, so you don't miss the next

225
00:14:09,494 --> 00:14:09,886
lesson.

226
00:14:10,336 --> 00:14:11,168
See you there.

